export const content = {"overview":{"label":"Whole sea lion","title":"Meet the California sea lion","young":"Sea lions live along the west coast of North America. They swim in the sea and rest on beaches and docks. Our sea lion is a grown-up male. He barks like a dog!","older":"California sea lions are eared seals from the West Coast of North America, from southeast Alaska to central Mexico. They live in shallow coastal water and rest on beaches, docks, buoys and jetties. They are playful, clever and very noisy, and they sound like barking dogs. Our model is a grown male: big, dark brown, with a raised bump on top of his head.","sources":["noaa","noaaseal"]},"hotspots":{"ears":{"label":"Ear flaps","title":"Little ear flaps","young":"See the tiny flaps on his head? Those are his ears. True seals have only ear holes. Ear flaps are how you can tell a sea lion from a seal.","older":"Sea lions are called eared seals because they have small ear flaps you can see. True seals have ear holes but no outer flap. The flaps are small, but a sea lion hears well both above and below the water. When it dives, its ears close.","sources":["noaaseal","smithsonian","noaa"]},"crest":{"label":"Crest","title":"A bump on his head","young":"Grown-up males have a high bump on top of the head, called a crest. The hair on it often turns light. Females have no big crest.","older":"As a male grows up, a bony crest grows along the top of his skull. It gives him a high, domed forehead. On most males the crest gets lighter in colour with age. Females have a thinner head that slopes gently down to the nose. Males are much bigger than females, too.","sources":["smithsonian","nps","noaa","tmmc"]},"body":{"label":"Body","title":"Shaped like a torpedo","young":"His body is smooth and shaped like a torpedo. It is big at the shoulders and thin at the tail. A layer of fat called blubber keeps him warm.","older":"A sea lion has a streamlined, torpedo-shaped body with strong, bulky shoulders that tapers to the tail. Under the skin is a thick layer of fat called blubber. It keeps the body warm and smooth in the water. Grown males are mostly dark brown. Females and young sea lions are blonde to tan.","sources":["smithsonian","seaworld","noaa"]},"foreflippers":{"label":"Front flippers","title":"Flippers that row","young":"Sea lions swim with their long front flippers. They beat them up and down like wings. Seals do it the other way and swim with their back flippers.","older":"The front flippers are long, dark and shaped like wings. A sea lion beats them up and down to row itself through the water, and steers with its back flippers. True seals swim the other way, by moving their back flippers back and forth, the way a fish uses its tail.","sources":["smithsonian","seaworld","noaaseal","nps"]},"hindflippers":{"label":"Back flippers","title":"Back flippers that walk","young":"In the water his back flippers help him steer, like a boat's rudder. On land he turns them forward under his body. Then he can walk on all four flippers!","older":"In the sea, the back flippers help steer, like a boat's rudder. On land, a sea lion turns them forward under its body and walks on all four flippers. True seals move on their bellies instead, like a caterpillar. The back flippers have small claws that sea lions use for grooming.","sources":["noaaseal","smithsonian"]}},"size":{"title":"Longer than a grown-up is tall","young":"A grown male sea lion is about 2.3 metres long. That is longer than a grown-up is tall! Females are smaller, about 1.8 metres. Ours is a male.","older":"NOAA says males grow to about 7.5 feet (2.3 metres) and 700 pounds (320 kilograms). Females grow to about 6 feet (1.8 metres) and 240 pounds (110 kilograms). Other sources give a little more or a little less. A newborn pup is about 75 centimetres long and weighs 13 to 20 pounds (6 to 9 kilograms). Our model is a grown male, 2.25 metres long.","sources":["noaa","smithsonian","tmmc","nps"]},"actions":{"porpoise":{"label":"Porpoise","short":"Porpoise"}},"captions":{"porpoise":{"young":"Whoosh! He jumps out of the water and dives back in. This is called porpoising. It may help him swim faster.","older":"Sea lions are sometimes seen porpoising: leaping out of the water as they swim, probably to go faster. Their front flippers do the work. How high our sea lion jumps is our guess."},"lightsOff":{"young":"Lights off! He can still find fish. His whiskers feel fish moving close by, even in the dark.","older":"Sea lions have good eyes for dim water. Even in total darkness, their sensitive whiskers can turn forward and feel the tiny movements of fish swimming nearby."}},"sound":{"label":"Hear sea lions bark","short":"Listen","young":"These are real sea lions on a beach in California. You hear them barking up in the air, not under the water. Sea lions bark like dogs.","older":"A real recording of a group of about 100 California sea lions on a sandy beach at Channel Islands Harbor, California, shared by Charla on iNaturalist. It was made in the air, on land, not under water, and plays at normal speed. Male sea lions bark like dogs to talk to other males and to females."},"sources":{"noaa":{"name":"NOAA Fisheries: California Sea Lion","what":"Size, weight, colours of males, females and pups, ear flaps, food, barking, range","url":"https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/species/california-sea-lion"},"noaaseal":{"name":"NOAA Fisheries: What's the difference between seals and sea lions?","what":"Ear flaps, how sea lions and true seals swim and move on land, the bark","url":"https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/feature-story/it-seal-or-sea-lion"},"nps":{"name":"US National Park Service, Channel Islands: California Sea Lion","what":"Size, the male crest, colours, flippers and tail, breeding","url":"https://www.nps.gov/chis/learn/nature/sea-lion.htm"},"smithsonian":{"name":"Smithsonian's National Zoo: California sea lion","what":"Body shape, ears, flippers, walking on land, whiskers, eyes, the crest, newborn pups","url":"https://nationalzoo.si.edu/animals/california-sea-lion"},"tmmc":{"name":"The Marine Mammal Center: California Sea Lion","what":"Size, the crest, porpoising, food, pups","url":"https://www.marinemammalcenter.org/animal-care/learn-about-marine-mammals/pinnipeds/california-sea-lion"},"tmmcguide":{"name":"The Marine Mammal Center: Your Visual Guide to Sea Lion and Seal Behavior","what":"Porpoising","url":"https://www.marinemammalcenter.org/news/your-visual-guide-to-sea-lion-and-seal-behavior"},"seaworld":{"name":"SeaWorld: All About the California Sea Lion, Adaptations","what":"Blubber, swimming strokes, diving depth","url":"https://seaworld.org/animals/all-about/california-sea-lion/adaptations"},"iucn":{"name":"IUCN Red List: Zalophus californianus","what":"Conservation status (Least Concern, 2014 assessment)","url":"https://www.iucnredlist.org/species/41666/45230310"},"inat":{"name":"iNaturalist observation 145868221, by Charla","what":"The recording: where, when and what it is","url":"https://www.inaturalist.org/observations/145868221"}}};
